GAP9: pv.unpack lane order and unpack2's register-pair destination

On GAP9 silicon pv.unpack1.lo.h.b.{s,u} gives {lane 0: ext(byte 0), lane 1:
ext(byte 1)}, and .hi the same for bytes 2 and 3. pv.unpack2.h.b.{s,u}
writes the register pair rd = unpack1.lo, rd+1 = unpack1.hi, and neither
reads nor writes the register in its rs2 field.

- unpack1_*: the RTL had the lanes the other way round, so GCC's own
  simplifications disagreed with the instruction: lane 0 of
  __builtin_pulp_unpacklo_h_b_s(x) became pv.extract.b of byte 1, and a
  constant argument folded to the swapped value.
- unpack2_*: modelled as a second SET into the rs2-field operand. Silicon
  writes rd+1 instead, which GCC didn't know, so a value it kept there was
  lost (pv.unpack2 a0,a0,a5 followed by a read of a1). unpack2_h_b_*_pair
  now sets a DImode register pair (an unspec) and prints zero in the rs2
  field; the builtin's expander returns the low half. The builtin's second
  argument has no effect, as before.

